.TH Q 1 "August 2026" "q 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
q, schedule \- command line queue, execution, and scheduling tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B q
.RB [ \-l | \-\-list ]
.br
.B q
.RB [ \-k | \-\-kill ]
.I job_id
.br
.B q
.RB [ \-L | \-\-logs ]
.I job_id
.br
.B q
.RB [ \-s | \-\-schedule ]
.RB [ \-l | \-\-list ]
.br
.B q
.RB [ \-s | \-\-schedule ]
.RB [ \-k | \-\-kill ]
.I schedule_id
.br
.B q
.RB [ \-s | \-\-schedule ]
.I timespec
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.br
.B schedule
.RB [ \-l | \-\-list ]
.br
.B schedule
.RB [ \-k | \-\-kill ]
.I schedule_id
.br
.B schedule
.I timespec
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.br
.B q
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.br
.B q
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B q
is a lightweight utility that queues command execution and runs jobs in parallel in the background, managed by a background daemon
.BR qdaemon .
It also serves as an asynchronous cron daemon and periodic command scheduler in userland.
If the daemon is not running when a command is queued, scheduled, or listed, the client starts it automatically.
.PP
.B schedule
is an alias for
.BR "q \-\-schedule" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List all jobs currently in the queue, including their ID, status, PID, start time, elapsed duration, and command. If combined with
.BR \-s " or " \-\-schedule ,
lists all scheduled commands along with their last run time, elapsed time since last run, next scheduled run, and command.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-kill " \fIid\fR"
Kill a running job or cancel a queued job. When combined with
.BR \-s " or " \-\-schedule " (or using " schedule "),"
removes the scheduled command with the specified ID.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-logs " \fIjob_id\fR"
Print the captured standard output (stdout) and standard error (stderr) of the specified job.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-schedule
Enable scheduling mode. When followed by a timespec and a command, schedules the command for periodic or cron execution. When used without a command or with
.BR \-l ", lists scheduled commands. When used with " \-k ,
removes a scheduled command.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-notify
Force sending a desktop notification when the command finishes, regardless of its duration.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-notify
Disable desktop notification for the command when it finishes.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of command usage and options.
.SH TIMESPEC FORMATS
The scheduler supports multiple timespec formats:
.TP
.B Cron Expressions
Standard 5-field cron syntax:
.I "minute hour day_of_month month day_of_week"
(e.g., \fB"0 12 * * *"\fR, \fB"*/5 * * * *"\fR, \fB"0 0 * * 1-5"\fR, \fB"0 10 * * Wed"\fR).
Standard cron shortcuts like \fB@hourly\fR, \fB@daily\fR, \fB@midnight\fR, \fB@weekly\fR, \fB@monthly\fR, \fB@yearly\fR are also supported.
.TP
.B Human-Readable Strings
Natural language scheduling expressions such as:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB"Wed 10 am"\fR, \fB"Wednesday 10:30 pm"\fR, \fB"every Wednesday at 10 am"\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB"weekdays at 8:00 am"\fR, \fB"weekends at 10 am"\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB"daily at 10 am"\fR, \fB"daily at 14:30"\fR, \fB"10 am"\fR, \fB"14:00"\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB"Mon,Wed,Fri 9am"\fR
.RE
.TP
.B Periodic Intervals
Fixed time periods such as \fB"every 5 hours"\fR, \fB"every 30 minutes"\fR, \fB"every 1 day"\fR, \fB"every 10 seconds"\fR, or shorthand notation like \fB"5h"\fR, \fB"30m"\fR, \fB"10s"\fR, \fB"2d"\fR.
.SH ASYNCHRONOUS CATCH-UP
The scheduler operates asynchronously. If the machine was powered off or the daemon was inactive when a scheduled command was due, the daemon automatically detects missed runs upon startup and executes them to catch up.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The daemon reads its configuration from a TOML file. The path is searched in the following order:
.IP 1.
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/q/q.conf
(usually
.IR ~/.config/q/q.conf )
.IP 2.
.I ~/.q/q.conf
.PP
The configuration options are:
.TP
.B max_parallel_jobs
The maximum number of jobs allowed to run concurrently (default is 2).
.TP
.B max_completed_jobs
The maximum number of finished jobs retained in the history/spool directory (default is 50).
.TP
.B enable_notifications
Boolean flag to enable or disable desktop notifications globally (default is true). Uses D-Bus on Linux/Unix and Toast Notifications on Windows.
.TP
.B min_notify_duration_secs
Minimum execution duration in seconds required for a completed command to trigger a desktop notification (default is 10).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.q/
Directory containing daemon runtime state.
.TP
.I ~/.q/q.sock
Unix domain socket used for client-daemon communication.
.TP
.I ~/.q/qdaemon.pid
Process ID of the running daemon.
.TP
.I ~/.q/spool/
Spool directory containing metadata and logs for each queued job.
.TP
.I ~/.q/schedules/
Directory containing specifications and tracking state for scheduled commands.
